1
00:00:00,220 --> 00:00:03,308
Every day, sunlight lands on your roof.

2
00:00:03,012 --> 00:00:05,402
Most of it turns into heat and

3
00:00:05,402 --> 00:00:08,224
drifts away. A solar panel catches that

4
00:00:08,224 --> 00:00:10,542
light and turns part of it into

5
00:00:10,542 --> 00:00:11,772
electricity.

6
00:00:11,720 --> 00:00:14,793
Inside the panel sit many silicon cells.

7
00:00:14,497 --> 00:00:17,539
When light particles, called photons, hit the

8
00:00:17,539 --> 00:00:20,500
silicon, they knock electrons loose.

9
00:00:20,512 --> 00:00:24,023
The panel produces direct current, which always

10
00:00:24,023 --> 00:00:26,998
flows one way. Your home needs alternating

11
00:00:26,998 --> 00:00:30,539
current that changes direction many times a

12
00:00:30,539 --> 00:00:32,904
second. An inverter makes that change.

13
00:00:32,928 --> 00:00:35,357
Power you do not use right away

14
00:00:35,357 --> 00:00:38,113
is either stored in a battery or

15
00:00:38,113 --> 00:00:39,933
sent into the grid. That way the

16
00:00:39,933 --> 00:00:42,554
lights still work after sunset.

17
00:00:42,595 --> 00:00:45,474
A typical panel turns roughly one fifth

18
00:00:45,474 --> 00:00:48,992
of the sunlight hitting it into electricity;

19
00:00:48,992 --> 00:00:51,328
the rest becomes heat. More panels, and

20
00:00:51,328 --> 00:00:53,326
a clear view of the sky, give

21
00:00:53,326 --> 00:00:54,547
you more power.

22
00:00:54,512 --> 00:00:57,798
Light in, electrons moving, an inverter shaping

23
00:00:57,798 --> 00:01:00,754
the current, and a battery holding it:

24
00:01:00,754 --> 00:01:03,265
that is how a solar panel powers

25
00:01:03,265 --> 00:01:03,942
a home.
